/// Chapter 39 of the guide, written from md-docs/recipes/javascript-interop.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page InteropGuide(path: "/docs/guide/interop", title: "JavaScript interop", description: "Your own .js under src/, libraries via meta.scripts, mount and cleanup on any element, Portal, wf:render, Element for custom elements, and publishing yours.", layout: DocsShell("JavaScript interop", chapter: t("nav.group.outputs"), blurb: "Write plain JavaScript beside your pages and call it by name; load a library from a CDN; hand an element to a script; place custom elements; publish your components.", source: "md-docs/recipes/javascript-interop.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "before-you-start", label: "Before you start"), Anchor(id: "call-your-own-script", label: "Call your own script"), Anchor(id: "load-a-library-from-a-cdn", label: "Load a library from a CDN"), Anchor(id: "hand-an-element-to-a-script-with-mount-update-and-cleanup", label: "Hand an element to a script with mount:, update: and cleanup:"), Anchor(id: "draw-into-a-node-a-script-made-with-portal", label: "Draw into a node a script made with Portal"), Anchor(id: "listen-for-wf-render-in-a-script-that-finds-elements-itself", label: "Listen for wf:render in a script that finds elements itself"), Anchor(id: "place-a-custom-element-from-a-library", label: "Place a custom element from a library"), Anchor(id: "publish-your-components-as-custom-elements", label: "Publish your components as custom elements"), Anchor(id: "call-wf-from-a-script-of-your-own", label: "Call WF from a script of your own"), Anchor(id: "migrate-from-external", label: "Migrate from external")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/server-rendering", prevLabel: t("ch.38"), nextTo: "/docs/reference", nextLabel: t("ch.40"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter shows you how to use JavaScript from a WebFluent project: your own scripts, libraries from a CDN, and custom elements. WebFluent has no package manager of its own and does not need one. A `.js` file is part of the project the same way a `.css` file is: put it under `src/`, and your pages can call its functions by name.\n\nPick the section for what you want to do:")

        Table(caption: "JavaScript interop") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("You want to…").header
                    Table.Cell("Use").header
                    Table.Cell("Section").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("call JavaScript of your own")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("a `.js` file under `src/`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Call your own script](#call-your-own-script)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("use a library from a CDN")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`meta.scripts` in the config") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Load a library from a CDN](#load-a-library-from-a-cdn)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("hand an element to a script, and take it back")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`mount:`, `update:`, `cleanup:` on any element") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Hand an element to a script](#hand-an-element-to-a-script-with-mount-update-and-cleanup)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("draw into a node a script made (a tooltip, a popup)")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Portal(into: node) \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Draw into a node a script made](#draw-into-a-node-a-script-made-with-portal)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("let a script find elements itself")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the `wf:render` event") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Listen for `wf:render`](#listen-for-wf-render-in-a-script-that-finds-elements-itself)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("place somebody else's custom element")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Element(\"tag-name\", …)`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Place a custom element](#place-a-custom-element-from-a-library)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("use your components from React, Vue or a plain page")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`output_type: \"elements\"`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Publish your components](#publish-your-components-as-custom-elements)") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Before you start", id: "before-you-start").h2

        Markdown("You need a WebFluent project ([Getting started](/docs/guide/getting-started)) and a script written as a plain browser script: no `import`, no `export`, no build step.")

        Heading("Call your own script", id: "call-your-own-script").h2

        Markdown("To call a function of your own, put it in a `.js` file under `src/`. Write it the way you would write any file a `<script src>` loads.\n\nThis script tilts an element toward the pointer, and returns a handle the page can update and destroy:")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/tilt.js
/**
 * Tilt an element toward the pointer.
 * @param {HTMLElement} node
 * @param {number} [max] how far, in degrees
 */
function initTilt(node, max = 15) {
  const move = (e) => {
    const r = node.getBoundingClientRect();
    const x = (e.clientX - r.left) / r.width - 0.5;
    node.style.transform = `rotateY(${x * max}deg)`;
  };
  node.addEventListener("pointermove", move);
  return {
    setMax(m) { max = m; },
    destroy() { node.removeEventListener("pointermove", move); },
  };
}"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("This page calls `initTilt` on a card, and changes how far it tilts as the reader moves a slider:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Gallery(path: "/gallery", title: "Gallery", description: "Cards that lean.") {
    state max = 15

    Heading("Gallery").h1
    Card(class: "tilt",
         mount: (n) => initTilt(n, max),
         update: (tilt) => tilt.setMax(max),
         cleanup: (tilt) => tilt.destroy()) {
        Text("Move the pointer over me.")
    }
    Slider(bind: max, min: 0, max: 45, label: "How far")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("How the build treats a script:\n\n- **The file runs as written.** The build copies it to `build/js/tilt.js` byte for byte and links it with `<script defer>` on every page, before the compiled code. A stack trace points at your own line.\n- **Its names are in scope.** Every top-level `function`, `class`, `const`, `let` and `var`, and anything assigned to `window.x`, is a name your `.wf` code can call directly. What a function or an IIFE keeps inside stays private, as it does in the browser.\n- **A call is checked.** `initTilt(n, 1, 2)` is a `T10` error where it is written, because the function takes one or two arguments. Its JSDoc types the arguments ([Type a script with JSDoc](#type-a-script-with-jsdoc)). A name the file does not declare is still a `T13`.\n- **A class is constructed.** WebFluent has no `new`. A call to a class a script declares compiles to `new`.\n- **One name has one meaning.** Two scripts that declare the same name, or a script name that is also a component, a store, a `const`, or a name the language or the browser owns (`format`, `log`, `fetch`), is an error that names both.\n- **An ES module is refused.** A file with a top-level `import` or `export` cannot run from a `<script src>`, so the build stops and says so. Load a published module from the config instead ([Load a library from a CDN](#load-a-library-from-a-cdn)).\n\nThe tools know your scripts too: `wf serve` rebuilds when a script changes, `wf audit` lists what each one declares, `wf types` prints each function's signature, and the editor completes, documents and jumps to them.")

        Heading("Type a script with JSDoc", id: "type-a-script-with-jsdoc").h3

        Markdown("To have calls checked against a function's types, describe the function in a JSDoc comment. JSDoc is a comment, so the file stays exactly what the browser runs. The checker reads it this way:")

        Table(caption: "Type a script with JSDoc") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("JSDoc").header
                    Table.Cell("WebFluent").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`string` · `number` · `boolean`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`String` · `Number` · `Bool`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`object` · `\{ a: T \}`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Map`, or a shape of the fields written") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T[]` · `Array<T>`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`[T]`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`?T` · `T | null` · `[name]`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T?`, or a parameter a call may leave out") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Promise<T>`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T`, as an action that awaits it reads it") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`(v: T) => R`") }
                    Table.Cell("a function: you may pass an action or a lambda")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`HTMLElement`, anything else") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Any`") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("With no comment at all, every parameter is `Any`, so adding a script never breaks a build. Each tag only narrows what a call may pass. `@returns` types the result.")

        Heading("Let a script report, and keep the class in WebFluent", id: "let-a-script-report-and-keep-the-class-in-webfluent").h3

        Markdown("When the way an element looks depends on something only a script can see (an intersection, a scroll position, a library's event), hand the script an action. An action is a plain function at run time. The script calls it, and the class stays in a `class:` the compiler can read.\n\nThis script reports whether an element is on screen:")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/visible.js
/**
 * Say whether `node` is on screen, each time that changes.
 * @param {HTMLElement} node
 * @param {(v: boolean) => void} onChange
 */
function watchVisible(node, onChange) {
  const io = new IntersectionObserver(([e]) => onChange(e.isIntersecting));
  io.observe(node);
  return { destroy: () => io.disconnect() };
}"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("This page sets `is-visible` on the card while it is on screen:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Story(path: "/story", title: "Story", description: "It fades in.") {
    state visible = false
    action setVisible(v: Bool) { visible = v }

    Heading("Story").h1
    Card(class: { "is-visible": visible },
         mount: (n) => watchVisible(n, setVisible),
         cleanup: (w) => w.destroy()) {
        Text("Here I am.")
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Each class has one owner. A class that `class:` names belongs to WebFluent, and a class a script adds with `classList.add` belongs to the script: `class:` only ever takes off what it put on.")

        Heading("Load a library from a CDN", id: "load-a-library-from-a-cdn").h2

        Markdown("To use a library from another origin, list its URL in `meta.scripts`. The compiler never fetches anything, so it cannot read a file on another origin; your own scripts call the library instead.\n\nThis config loads Chart.js and pins the file with its integrity hash:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "meta": {
    "scripts": ["https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/chart.js@4.4.1/dist/chart.umd.min.js"],
    "integrity": {
      "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/chart.js@4.4.1/dist/chart.umd.min.js": "sha384-…"
    }
} }"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("This script draws a chart with it:")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/charts.js
/**
 * A bar chart of `values`, one bar a quarter.
 * @param {HTMLCanvasElement} canvas
 * @param {number[]} values
 */
function drawSales(canvas, values) {
  return new Chart(canvas, {
    type: "bar",
    data: { labels: ["Q1", "Q2", "Q3", "Q4"], datasets: [{ label: "Sales", data: values }] },
  });
}"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("This page hands the script a canvas, and destroys the chart when the page leaves:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Sales(path: "/sales", title: "Sales", description: "How it is going.") {
    state values: [Number] = [12, 19, 7, 15]

    Heading("Sales").h1
    Host(tag: "canvas",
         mount: (c) => drawSales(c, values),
         cleanup: (chart) => chart.destroy())
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("How an entry in `meta.scripts` loads:\n\n- **In order.** The build links each entry with `<script defer>` in the order listed, before your own scripts and the compiled code. A library is there when the code that uses it runs.\n- **Out of order.** `\{ \"src\": \"…\", \"async\": true \}` loads a script that nothing on the page waits for (analytics, a tag manager) with `<script async>`. Deferred scripts run in order, so a large one listed plainly would hold back the compiled code, and the page's first full paint, until it arrived. An `async` script runs whenever it arrives instead. A module, or a script whose `globals` your `.wf` code calls, cannot be `async` (`E111`).\n- **After the page.** `\{ \"src\": \"…\", \"load\": \"after\" \}` puts no tag in the HTML at all: the runtime requests the script once the page has loaded and drawn its first frame. A browser fetches everything a page names while it loads, and a page's speed is measured against what it asked for before it first painted. A large analytics tag named in the HTML can cost a phone's Largest Contentful Paint more than a second, even with `async`. The script's origin still joins the policy. A reader who leaves within the first second is not counted. The same limits as `async` apply (`E111`).\n- **Pinned.** `meta.integrity` pins the file to a hash. A library from another origin without a hash draws a `D05` warning.\n- **Allowed by the policy.** The build widens the Content-Security-Policy by exactly the origins listed, so the policy that ships beside a declared library never blocks it.\n- **As a module.** `\{ \"src\": \"https://…/confetti.mjs\", \"module\": true, \"as\": \"Confetti\" \}` imports an ES module and puts its exports on `window.Confetti`. Most CDNs also serve a plain build, which needs neither key.\n- **Called from `.wf` directly.** `\{ \"src\": \"…\", \"globals\": [\"Chart\"] \}` puts the names a library defines in scope, each typed `Any`, because the compiler cannot see their shape. A class among them is constructed when called.")

        Heading("Hand an element to a script with mount:, update: and cleanup:", id: "hand-an-element-to-a-script-with-mount-update-and-cleanup").h2

        Markdown("To give an element to a library and take it back when the element goes, use the three lifetime props. Every element and every component call takes them:\n\n- `mount: (node) => …` runs each time the element is made: the first paint, a route change, an `if` branch arriving, a list item added. What it returns is the *handle* the other two receive.\n- `update: (handle) => …` runs again whenever the state it reads changes.\n- `cleanup: (handle) => …` runs when the element leaves (its page, its branch or its list item), so nothing a library made outlives it.\n\nOn a component call, the three belong to the component's root element, unless the component declares a prop of that name itself. A `mount:` that throws removes itself and logs the error; the page stays up.\n\nTo get a bare node for a library, use `Host`. It has the same lifetime: `Host(tag: \"canvas\", …)` makes the element the library wants. It makes a `div` by default, or a `span`, `canvas`, `svg`, `section`, `figure`, `pre`, `p`, `ul` or `table`.")

        Heading("Draw into a node a script made with Portal", id: "draw-into-a-node-a-script-made-with-portal").h2

        Markdown("Some libraries make a node and leave it empty for the page to fill: an editor's tooltip, a map's popup, a chart's legend. To fill it, use `Portal(into: node) \{ … \}`. The library keeps the node's place, its keys and its focus; the page draws what is in it.\n\nThis script starts a CodeMirror editor, loaded from `meta.scripts` as `CM`, with a hover tooltip whose words the page draws:")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/editor.js
// CodeMirror places each tooltip and keeps its keys; the node it makes is
// left empty, and the page draws what is in it.
const tooltips = new Map();
let made = 0;

/**
 * The node CodeMirror made for a tooltip, or null once it has gone.
 * @param {string} id
 * @returns {HTMLElement|null}
 */
function editorHost(id) {
  return tooltips.get(id) ?? null;
}

/**
 * An editor in `node`. `opened(id, text)` hears a tooltip open, with the
 * words under the pointer; `closed(id)` hears it close.
 * @param {HTMLElement} node
 * @param {(id: string, text: string) => void} opened
 * @param {(id: string) => void} closed
 */
function startEditor(node, opened, closed) {
  const hover = CM.hoverTooltip((view, pos) => ({
    pos,
    create() {
      const id = `hover-${++made}`;
      const dom = document.createElement("div");
      tooltips.set(id, dom);
      opened(id, view.state.doc.lineAt(pos).text);
      return { dom, destroy() { tooltips.delete(id); closed(id); } };
    },
  }));
  return new CM.EditorView({ parent: node, extensions: [CM.basicSetup, hover] });
}"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("This page keeps the open tooltip's id in state and draws the words into its node:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Edit(path: "/edit", title: "Edit", description: "An editor whose tooltip the page draws.") {
    state tip: String? = null
    state words = ""
    action opened(id: String, text: String) {
        tip = id
        words = text
    }
    action closed(id: String) {
        if tip == id { tip = null }
    }

    Heading("Edit").h1
    Host(mount: (n) => startEditor(n, opened, closed), cleanup: (view) => view.destroy())
    if let id = tip {
        Portal(into: editorHost(id), data-popup: "hover") {
            Text(words)
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("How a portal behaves:\n\n- **It reads its target again.** `into:` is read like any other expression: when a state or a list item it reads changes, it is read again. A tooltip that replaces another has a new id, so the block follows it to the new node.\n- **It moves; it is not drawn again.** When `into:` names another node, the same box moves there with its nodes and their state: a field half typed in, a list scrolled halfway. `null` keeps the box aside, and when a node is named again the same nodes come back.\n- **It belongs to where it is written.** Its handlers, its `emit`, the state it reads and changes, and its `mount:` and `cleanup:` belong to the page or the component it is written in. The node is only where they are drawn. A `mount:` inside the block runs once the block is drawn and in place, so it meets its node in the document when the node it goes into is there.\n- **It leaves with its scope.** When the page, the branch or the list item it is written in leaves, the box leaves the node with it. The node belongs to the library, and stays.\n- **The box adds nothing to the layout.** It is a `div` with `display: contents`, so the node lays out the block as if the box were not there. An `id:`, a `class:`, `data-*` and `aria-*` go on the box.\n- **It can target a handle.** `Portal(into: panel)` draws into the element a `ref: panel` names, even one written further down the page. Where it goes is read once more when the drawing is over and the page is in the document, so an element of the page's own that `document.getElementById` finds is found too. A handle is not a state: an element the handle names only later, in an `if` that opens afterwards, is not followed.\n- **Its events bubble from where it is drawn.** A press inside the block bubbles through the node it is in and that node's parents, not through the elements around the place it is written. An `on click` on a `Stack` that holds the portal does not hear it. Put the handler on the portal, or inside its block.\n- **`show` does not hide it.** `show` hides what is drawn in its own place, and a portal's block is drawn somewhere else. Remove it with `if`, or keep it aside with `into: null`.\n- **The pre-rendered page has only a marker.** The *static paint* (the HTML the build pre-renders for a page) has a marker where a portal is, and nothing of its block. The live page draws the block once its script has run. A test that looks at a portal runs in a browser ([Testing](/docs/guide/testing#test-a-portal)).\n\nA list that the library keeps (an editor's completions, each row a node it made) is a keyed `for` of portals. A press on a row should leave the focus in the editor: `on mousedown(e) \{ e.preventDefault() \}` does that, and `on click` still runs. This fragment draws one portal per row:")

        CodeBlock(#"for row, i in Completions.rows by i {
    Portal(into: editorHost(row.id), data-popup: "row") {
        Text(row.label) {
            on mousedown(e) { e.preventDefault() }
            on click { Completions.pick(i) }
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A `Portal` without `into:` is a `C01` error.")

        Heading("Listen for wf:render in a script that finds elements itself", id: "listen-for-wf-render-in-a-script-that-finds-elements-itself").h2

        Markdown("The page is drawn after your scripts run, drawn again over a pre-rendered page, and drawn anew on every route. A script that runs its own `querySelectorAll` therefore listens for the drawing to land, with the `wf:render` event on `document`:")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/tilt-all.js
document.addEventListener("wf:render", (e) => {
  // e.detail.route is the route just drawn, e.detail.params its parameters.
  for (const el of document.querySelectorAll(".tilt-me:not([data-tilted])")) {
    el.dataset.tilted = "";
    initTilt(el);
  }
});"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("`wf:render` fires once per drawing: the first mount, the takeover of a pre-rendered page, and every route. It does not fire for an `if` branch or a list item that arrives later. Use `mount:` for those.")

        Heading("Place a custom element from a library", id: "place-a-custom-element-from-a-library").h2

        Markdown("To place a custom element that a library defines, use `Element` with the element's tag. This page places Stripe's pricing table and notes when it is ready:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Pricing(path: "/pricing", title: "Pricing", description: "Plans.") {
    state loaded = false
    Heading("Pricing").h1
    Element("stripe-pricing-table", publishableKey: "pk_live_…", pricing-table-id: "prctbl_1") {
        on ready { loaded = true }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- The tag comes first. It is lower case, with a hyphen, as every custom element's tag is; any other tag is an `E116` error.\n- A named argument is an attribute: `publishableKey` is written `publishable-key`. An attribute follows state like any other value.\n- An `on` handler hears the events the element fires, whatever their names.\n- The block is the element's children.\n- `class:`, `style \{ \}` and `mount:` work as on any other element.\n\nThe script that defines the element (your own, or one in `meta.scripts`) upgrades it when it runs.")

        Heading("Publish your components as custom elements", id: "publish-your-components-as-custom-elements").h2

        Markdown("To use your components from another framework or a plain page, build them as custom elements. Name the components in `build.elements`:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "build": { "output_type": "elements", "elements": ["PriceTag", "Rating"] } }"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("Each name becomes a standard custom element. `PriceTag` is `<price-tag>`. A one-word component gets a prefix, so its tag has the hyphen a custom element needs: `Rating` is `<wf-rating>`. The build writes `elements.js`, `styles.css`, and a page that lists the tags it published.\n\nThis HTML places a published component on any page:")

        CodeBlock(#"<script src="/elements.js" defer></script>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles.css">

<price-tag label="Pro" amount="29" sale></price-tag>"#, title: "html")

        Markdown("How a published element behaves:\n\n- **An attribute is a prop.** It is read when the element is connected, and again whenever it changes. A `Number` prop is read as a number, and a `Map` or a list as JSON.\n- **A `Bool` prop is true when the attribute is present**, as HTML reads `disabled`, and false when it is `=\"false\"`, so a framework that writes the string still works.\n- **An event the component declares** is dispatched as a `CustomEvent` that bubbles.\n- **What the component created goes** when the element leaves the document.\n\nReact, Vue, Svelte, Angular, Rails, WordPress and a plain page can all place a tag. That is why WebFluent publishes custom elements rather than an adapter per framework: the interface every framework shares is the web platform itself.")

        Heading("Call WF from a script of your own", id: "call-wf-from-a-script-of-your-own").h2

        Markdown("The runtime is `window.WF`. A script under `src/`, the console, or a library's callback can call it: `WF.navigate(\"/about\")`, `WF.toast(\"Saved\", \"success\")`, `WF.storeSnapshot(\"Cart\")`. The [runtime reference](/docs/guide/runtime) lists every function.")

        Callout("A build keeps only the runtime modules its own code reaches. A script that calls a module nothing else uses needs `\"build\": \{ \"runtime\": \"full\" \}`, or the call fails in the browser.", kind: .warning)

        Markdown("A script from another origin must be listed in `meta.scripts`, which widens the policy for it. The build's CSP check refuses an inline `<script>` ([Security](/docs/guide/security#ship-a-content-security-policy)).")

        Heading("Migrate from external", id: "migrate-from-external").h2

        Markdown("Before 4.2, you described JavaScript with `external` declarations; 4.2 removed them. `wf migrate` rewrites a project:\n\n- A remote module becomes a `meta.scripts` entry. Its calls keep working, typed `Any`.\n- A local module becomes a plain script under `src/`, with its exports removed and its signatures as JSDoc. `X.f(…)` is rewritten to `f(…)`.\n- An `external element` call site becomes `Element(\"tag-name\", …)`.")
    }
}
